RAWMSTAT.CONF(5)

# NAME

rawmstat.conf - rawm native status source configuration

# DESCRIPTION

*rawmstat*(1) uses libconfig syntax. The top-level *sources* group describes
ordered named measurements. Presentation policy such as separators, colors,
hit testing, and click bindings belongs to *rawm*(1).

The built-in configuration contains one clock source. In normal mode rawmstat
uses the user file when present, otherwise the system file, otherwise built-in
defaults. With *rawmstat -c path*, _path_ is selected explicitly. Use
*rawmstat -C* to validate configuration without opening X.

The wire protocol is intentionally not configurable. rawm and rawmstat are
released together; incompatible protocol changes use a new root property and
codec rather than compatibility policy in this file.

# SOURCES

*sources* is a group. Each member name is the stable segment identifier and
must contain 1..64 characters from *A-Z*, *a-z*, *0-9*, period, underscore, or
hyphen. Group order is snapshot order.

```
sources = {
  cpu = {
    provider = "cpu";
    period_ms = 2000;
    warning = 80;
    critical = 95;
  };

  clock = {
    provider = "clock";
    period_ms = 30000;
    format = "%a %b %d %H:%M";
  };
};
```

Every source requires *provider*. *period_ms* is its monotonic refresh period,
in milliseconds, from 100 through 3600000.

## battery

Reads the first usable *BAT\** power-supply entry from Linux sysfs.

*warning* and *critical* are percentage thresholds. Defaults are 20 and 10 and
must satisfy *critical < warning*. Charging and full batteries remain normal.

## clock

Formats local wall-clock time with *strftime*(3). *format* is required after
configuration defaults are applied and defaults to *%a %b %d %H:%M*.

## cpu

Reads aggregate Linux CPU counters from _/proc/stat_ and computes utilization
from consecutive in-memory samples. The first sample establishes a baseline
and is not published.

*warning* and *critical* are utilization percentages. Defaults are 80 and 95
and must satisfy *warning < critical*.

## memory

Reads *MemTotal* and *MemAvailable* from _/proc/meminfo_. *warning* and
*critical* are used-memory percentages, defaulting to 85 and 95.

## filesystem

Uses *statvfs*(3) for *path*, which defaults to _/_. *warning* and *critical*
are available-space thresholds in GiB, defaulting to 20 and 5 and requiring
*critical < warning*.

## network

Reads Linux interface byte counters from sysfs and keeps the previous counters
and monotonic timestamp in memory. *interface* defaults to *"default"*, which
selects the interface carrying the IPv4 default route. A literal interface name
may be configured instead.

The first sample after startup, route change, counter rollback, or interface
change publishes the interface name without a rate. Later samples publish RX
and TX byte rates.

## exec

Runs a direct argument vector for measurements without a native provider.

*command* = (string, ...)
	Required non-empty argv vector. Shell syntax is not interpreted unless a
	shell is explicitly named.

*timeout_ms* = integer
	Maximum command lifetime, 1..600000 ms. Default 2000.

Only exit status 0 commits a sample. Only the first output line is used. Plain
text is *normal*. A leading *normal<TAB>*, *warning<TAB>*, or
*critical<TAB>* selects semantic state and is removed. Empty, malformed, or
non-printable output fails the refresh. *stale* is reserved for rawmstat's own
freshness state and is rejected as command-supplied semantics.

# FAILURE SEMANTICS

A failed refresh with no prior value produces no segment. If a source already
has a valid value, rawmstat keeps that text and changes its state to *stale*.
The next successful sample replaces both text and state.

The complete *\_RAWM\_STATUS\_V3* snapshot must fit within 4096 bytes. rawmstat
reports an oversized snapshot and keeps the previously published property
rather than truncating it.

# TEST FIXTURE ROOTS

For integration tests, native Linux providers honor *RAWMSTAT_PROC_ROOT* and
*RAWMSTAT_SYS_ROOT* in place of _/proc_ and _/sys_. These variables are test
interfaces, not desktop configuration policy.

# FILES

_/etc/rawm/rawmstat.conf_
	System configuration.

_$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/rawm/rawmstat.conf_
	Preferred user configuration when present.

_$HOME/.config/rawm/rawmstat.conf_
	Fallback user configuration.

# SEE ALSO

*rawmstat*(1), *rawm*(1), *rawm.conf*(5), *libconfig*(5)

